For grids where background cells selectively change to a third color, do not infer the rule from one visually salient adjacency (for example, two same-color markers with one background cell between them). In the task-0c08c3f2 interaction, two submissions based on local interpolation and broader hand-guessed propagation were incorrect. The supplied demos displayed much wider, selective regions of background-to-3 changes and also a no-op case with a lone non-background pixel; these observations do not validate either attempted test output.

For future recurrences, record exact input/output deltas for every demo, including unchanged markers and unchanged background regions. Compare explicit candidates such as row/column rays, marker-bounded intervals, and other structured propagation against all changed and unchanged examples. Keep these as hypotheses until one explains every demo. After an incorrect submission, discard the full proposed rule rather than making local tweaks. Use further demos to resolve named uncertainties, and preserve the benchmark's exact one-action JSON response contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-2/play/users/u054/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/selective-propagation-delta-discipline.md"}
